# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — Build Context Exclusions
# =============================================================================
#
# INTERVIEW CONTEXT:
# When you run `docker build`, the Docker CLI sends the entire build context
# (the directory specified in the build command or docker-compose.yml) to the
# Docker daemon as a tar archive. The daemon then executes the Dockerfile
# instructions against this context.
#
# .dockerignore works like .gitignore — it tells the CLI which files to
# EXCLUDE from the build context before sending it to the daemon.
#
# Why this matters:
#   1. PERFORMANCE: A smaller context is faster to send to the daemon,
#      especially with remote Docker daemons or in CI/CD. A .git directory
#      alone can be hundreds of MB.
#   2. SECURITY: Files in the build context are accessible to any COPY/ADD
#      instruction. If you don't exclude .env files, a careless COPY . .
#      could bake secrets into the image layer — visible to anyone who
#      pulls the image and inspects its layers (docker history, dive, etc.).
#   3. CACHE EFFICIENCY: Any file change in the context can invalidate
#      layers. Excluding volatile files (logs, editor swap files) prevents
#      unnecessary cache busting.
#
# Syntax is similar to .gitignore:
#   *         matches any sequence of non-separator characters
#   **        matches any number of directories (including zero)
#   ?         matches a single non-separator character
#   !         negation (re-include a previously excluded pattern)
#   #         comment
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# Version Control
# =============================================================================
# The .git directory contains the entire repository history, all branches,
# pack files, and metadata. It can easily be 10x the size of the actual
# source code. It is never needed inside a Docker build.
.git

# .gitignore is a development/repo concern, not needed in the image.
.gitignore


# =============================================================================
# Secrets and Environment Files
# =============================================================================
# CRITICAL: .env files often contain database passwords, API keys, tokens,
# and other secrets. If these end up in the build context, a COPY . .
# instruction will include them in an image layer — permanently.
#
# Even if you delete the file in a later layer, the secret remains in the
# earlier layer (Docker images are additive). Anyone with access to the
# image can extract it.
#
# Rule: NEVER let secret files enter the build context.
.env
.env.*
.env.local
.env.production


# =============================================================================
# Documentation
# =============================================================================
# Markdown files, license files, and other docs add to context size but
# are never used by the compiled Go binary at runtime.
*.md
LICENSE
LICENSE.*


# =============================================================================
# Build and Development Tooling
# =============================================================================
# Makefile and docker-compose files are used to BUILD or ORCHESTRATE
# containers — they don't belong inside the container itself.
Makefile
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*


# =============================================================================
# IDE and Editor Files
# =============================================================================
# Every developer uses different editors. These config directories and
# swap files are machine-specific and should never be in the image.
# They also change frequently, which would bust the Docker layer cache.
.vscode/
.idea/

# Vim swap files — created when a file is open in Vim/Neovim.
*.swp
*.swo
*~


# =============================================================================
# Go-Specific Exclusions
# =============================================================================
# vendor/:
#   If you're using Go modules (which you should be), the Dockerfile
#   runs `go mod download` to fetch dependencies into the module cache.
#   Including a local vendor/ directory would be redundant and large.
#   If you use `go mod vendor` + `GOFLAGS=-mod=vendor` in your Dockerfile,
#   then you'd REMOVE vendor/ from this ignore list.
vendor/

# Test binaries — produced by `go test -c`. Not needed in production.
*.test

# Coverage output files — produced by `go test -coverprofile`.
*.out
coverage/

# Compiled binary output — we build fresh inside Docker.
# If you also build locally (e.g., `go build -o bin/`), these artifacts
# shouldn't be sent to the daemon.
bin/
dist/


# =============================================================================
# OS-Generated Files
# =============================================================================
# macOS creates .DS_Store in every directory you open in Finder.
# Windows creates Thumbs.db for thumbnail caches.
# These are small but change unpredictably, causing cache invalidation.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db


# =============================================================================
# CI/CD Configuration
# =============================================================================
# GitHub Actions workflows, GitLab CI config, and similar files define
# CI/CD pipelines. They reference the Docker build but are not used
# inside it. Excluding them keeps the context clean.
.github/
.gitlab-ci.yml
.circleci/


# =============================================================================
# Miscellaneous
# =============================================================================
# Terraform, Helm charts, and other infrastructure-as-code files
# are deployment concerns, not application runtime concerns.
terraform/
helm/
charts/

# Temporary files
tmp/
*.tmp
*.log
